The river at this point was about a thousand feet wide. And they, too,were =
like Valkyries, each of them a shield-maiden of the Aesir. Then something a=
bout the marshalled ranks of black stones struck me.
I hadcovered half of it when I heard a shrill shriek and felt the buffeting=
of wings about my head.
And with it came angryresentment of her summons. Works on the respiratory c=
entres of thebrain and speeds up respiration.
It wasmore difficult than it had appeared; it took us two hours to wind our=
way to the top. But it was no soul of a slain warrior snatchedup for flight=
 to Valhalla.
I hurled the squirming thing into theboiling pool. Their breath came pulsin=
g up to me, jungle-strong,laden with the unfamiliar fragrances.
Oh, yes, this rockfall keeps right on going down. Inside the tower was a cr=
umbling flight of stones leading to its top.
The Wolf-womans eyes were like sapphirestars, her free hand was raised in s=
ummons.
The littlewoman stayed with us, nodding and smiling and patting our kneesre=
assuringly.
Anything might live in a place like that.
The slide ended abruptly at the edge of this waste.
The barrier led to the east, dippingsteadily downward.
I felt alongits edge and it seems to go on a bit further.
The pack and the pushingrifles should have been checked by that barrier at =
the slides end. If we camp at the base of those hills, well be out of that =
wind,said Jim. I caught himwith one hand, but it broke my own precarious ho=
ld. Over that silvery pelt her hair fell in twothick braids of flaming red.=
 It flowed among the black pyramids,half-submerging them, quenching the sac=
rificial smokes. The falcon circling round my headscreamed, and flew.
I stretched myself out on the slab, and reached out to touch it. We went fo=
rward, avoiding with difficultythe thorns.
Each and every one of them is a tinyduplicate of the mountain behind them!=
=20
